#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205125 :tutorial/content "Given a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ we know that its variance, which we may denote as $\\sigma_x^2,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_x^2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline x$ is the mean of $x.$ \n\nThe summation term\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nis known as a **sum of squares** and is denoted $S_{xx}.$ As we'll see, this quantity often appears in statistical analysis, so giving it a separate notation is useful.\n\nSimilarly, if we have a data set $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n,$ then we define\n\n$$\nS_{yy} =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\overline y)^2.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we have the bivariate data set\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1), \\qquad (x_2, y_2),\\qquad \\ldots, \\qquad (x_n, y_n).\n$$\n\nThen, the covariance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)(y_i - \\overline y)\n$$\n\nand we may define $S_{xy}$ as\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can define the variance and covariance in terms of $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n},\\,{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}=\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n},\\,\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7e454c5-fb32-5adc-b4bd-0eb93ccd4ce7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10575 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205126 :tutorial/content "Calculating $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ using their definitions can be cumbersome. Thankfully, we have the following shortcut formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2} \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2} \\\\ {S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "c497f58a-e56b-5fde-9a53-c8553e8dcbcf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10576 :tutorial/title "Shortcut Formulas"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205148 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that\n\n![](images/6e/6e45f9e7a628f5275b3da6d56cbf02f7b36a058ed330f92176c50a3820a950d7.svg)\n\nWe start with the definition of ![](images/35/3596d7dd81777a4d3334085c1b707f68c8ade782b3a9b435ec815b5b9cd718c1.svg)\n\n![](images/47/4729fc0ed523938607245027c4e2354b97d5c70fc12faba653f826123ce7e116.svg)\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbf3c03450c32af2eac42db8a67fa185cb7a88a48b9f55a22b21b8f15bbc8d0.svg)\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n![](images/07/079466ea370445b1274143031c8ddf28d09569757c05a439ff967d6c5e835dc5.svg)\n\nNow, since ![](images/41/413a174ed8eab4231f8960e47693592ddfebbf73fbad3284ccd11c5233076316.svg) and ![](images/ed/ed33df1256b85619e6408ee6014ce30223f75c5df8ca599ec4f4c99fe0f293ac.svg) are constants, we can write this as\n\n![](images/8a/8a12508a4a5e1890cac8b9814966b826dd67c7c89977e2fcb223b859ee016464.svg)\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n![](images/de/de94aad0fc385eebb157d4a4fec1f763ef02190462eacf53f27de4769f3ae42d.svg)\n\nThis gives\n\n![](images/98/98de282215c98d829d9d7984442773a2086ca640b29542382a060826270489ae.svg)\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for ![](images/40/409afd81c4ea93a33782db88296b0a9f7e9e982a14a16274e2ec50739d363f14.svg) and ![](images/02/02f6f964f68603338fc2cbe3b6d3be2b6af947670bec73bc8810b14208e1090e.svg) are similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "7dab2448-da48-5100-9373-89c074ae5326" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10852 :tutorial/title "Proving the Shortcut Formulas"}]}
